#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124337 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "efccacfd-9595-5315-86c7-de9fefd8ed1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5497" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\n- $(\\lambda-2)^2(\\lambda-3)=0$\n\n- $\\lambda^3-2\\lambda^2+3\\lambda=0$\n\n- $\\lambda^3-4\\lambda + 7=0$" :question/worked-solution "According to the invertible matrix theorem, if $A$ is invertible, then $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124338 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/id #uuid "d7619d9b-e5d7-55e5-96a8-0d05b3532620" :question/math-academy-id "e-5498" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix and $\\textrm{rank}(B+4I) = 3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) = \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$\n\n- $B +4I$ is singular\n\n- $\\lambda = -4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](images/62/6273b311b461cc16f516a4d9da6d928643e988c7847cfd14e23401a265d9e25b.png)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+4I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B+4I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0,$ we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124417 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "107369d4-980d-5d73-85ea-8fcf522b96f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8606" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has a nonzero solution\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) = 3$\n\n- $\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\ne\\{ \\mathbf{0}\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](images/fd/fd48d2627c161d3e82529aeeb42bcd830c2fc83a9c8d0b7522d1857f099d6f38.png)\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A,$ which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d4ff535-a1f0-5ce6-8b0f-0685c923aebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464138} {:answer/id #uuid "e9853f79-3460-5bbc-b09c-1486f81f69b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d4ff535-a1f0-5ce6-8b0f-0685c923aebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464138} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9e19efd-b193-5d8c-951e-06d2594d3c69" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d591cdfe-787c-561e-b1ab-96a237381b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464140} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd54853-c89a-5086-aedb-4b0387aa7aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bd54853-c89a-5086-aedb-4b0387aa7aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464141} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d9f0ad-5dbc-52a9-a14d-c0423c3659b8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d063335-7f55-523f-94d2-bc87bd49b8d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464142} {:answer/id #uuid "c08d56be-7940-5e02-8c76-d6c91fd825db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d063335-7f55-523f-94d2-bc87bd49b8d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464142} :answer-field/id #uuid "708f3344-420a-5f82-a998-358eeabd9c9b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27c4d64e-3a6c-52f2-bb70-03acd0cf6bbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140834" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n- **I.** $\\lambda (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)=0$\n- **II.** $2{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4=0$\n- **III.** $5{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b49fd5-f088-5454-be5d-5107c647f0f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449580} {:answer/id #uuid "13e0f499-69e4-5ec1-b4f2-1ad1e4f28df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13e0f499-69e4-5ec1-b4f2-1ad1e4f28df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449581} :answer-field/id #uuid "272bcebc-fe8b-57b4-ae53-626fe72f2b23" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450744} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e17838a-706f-5603-a10d-92de20c57c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449582} {:answer/id #uuid "0ab54959-e3f9-5e7c-b65b-3f4c44ab857d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e17838a-706f-5603-a10d-92de20c57c18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449582} :answer-field/id #uuid "c399833e-a33e-5bf1-abf9-d826969c4585" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450745} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62e97b3f-deab-5976-8ff6-07262066e30a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449584} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3e26df-93cc-5b9e-a97e-f3c98599c684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b3e26df-93cc-5b9e-a97e-f3c98599c684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449585} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1e96681-263d-592f-94b0-d87293b9e481" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1812a9f8-6cf3-5d8f-b5c7-1adf1d688a91" :question/math-academy-id "q-140835" :question/problem "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation $2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- **III.** $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 16-12-4 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the characteristic equation, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31698bf4-835e-5391-bd94-e5338ea6725e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449586} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3381fd-405f-5c82-9e7e-171f8dc5dbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d3381fd-405f-5c82-9e7e-171f8dc5dbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449587} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d2e4de-00be-5228-ac60-63ff9be610ee" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450747} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5889c469-8246-5988-a4b6-18db86a78f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449588} {:answer/id #uuid "4c685a84-65d1-5256-b2c2-aa1ebe137a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5889c469-8246-5988-a4b6-18db86a78f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449588} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dca00bd-3230-521f-ad18-be85e3bb3f7f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450748} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea8085ac-e799-5424-8198-147afdc52305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449590} {:answer/id #uuid "42e23434-b855-57e0-a5e5-feab08f636f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea8085ac-e799-5424-8198-147afdc52305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449590} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ee94435-98e2-554c-8d74-17c95d5c7950" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47d1848e-d69a-5675-a8fe-fca5e30be264" :question/math-academy-id "q-140838" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A+4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A+4I)={R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A+4I)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+4I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47e1bc98-e7e4-52f8-984d-07090fcce509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448608} {:answer/id #uuid "bd61a0f6-b4d4-5449-b479-f5e34f173f7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd61a0f6-b4d4-5449-b479-f5e34f173f7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448609} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cef80fb-6ab7-552e-906b-93d05bf07abc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab6b7cce-8578-54ae-a81c-3138c19f494d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448610} {:answer/id #uuid "4eaa0245-8571-54f0-b959-74a0a10d530d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eaa0245-8571-54f0-b959-74a0a10d530d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448611} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c3d2be-b0df-5bbf-8142-98edc40dc42f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449345} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0254d36b-a197-5a46-943b-99f03cefa2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448612} {:answer/id #uuid "4bbbfda1-4699-5f8e-b698-221bd7d85dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0254d36b-a197-5a46-943b-99f03cefa2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448612} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5503ea3-b219-5b46-b79b-0e271f4f1b90" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3be4d323-0e25-52c5-8eb8-5afe165a1c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-37523" :question/problem "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda =0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =3$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- **III.** $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =3$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (3)^{3}-(3)^{2}-6(3) & =0 \\\\ 27-9-18 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the characteristic equation. As a result, $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb1fef96-a4b5-56a3-ae12-1ad088781515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448702} {:answer/id #uuid "c988276e-ae24-58e1-b3c3-71288a68bb61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c988276e-ae24-58e1-b3c3-71288a68bb61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448703} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cfef4ec-bf41-5b26-a871-8eaa14e4c137" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fab0312d-2e7a-525e-ba6d-532165dc1bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448704} {:answer/id #uuid "46330574-7aae-5314-b20d-1471b582f872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fab0312d-2e7a-525e-ba6d-532165dc1bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448704} :answer-field/id #uuid "004390c1-de50-56c3-839a-de91fd3c41a3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449392} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7cdfa98-4044-5fa7-991a-cdd2d17905fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448706} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ebdbcd-7ff6-5904-beff-00069d922192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7cdfa98-4044-5fa7-991a-cdd2d17905fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448706} :answer-field/id #uuid "872a7bbb-a3ff-52ad-be3b-b42dbd804cf8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0e0c0ef-5198-55d8-9aeb-10b338580777" :question/math-academy-id "q-47568" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A-3I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A-3I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-3I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A-3I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A-3I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-3I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cc28d24-2966-5404-9657-12db361e583f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448834} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb9ebe2-2b9f-520b-ae5c-3a3b086533b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cc28d24-2966-5404-9657-12db361e583f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448834} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7a49a0-682e-5485-8704-c21a8df81e3a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449457} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8f791e7-bb86-5a9f-b7a4-d3cc7968a9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448836} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d857bb-3ddc-59a7-a9e1-b62a1bf5bbc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1d857bb-3ddc-59a7-a9e1-b62a1bf5bbc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448837} :answer-field/id #uuid "979415cb-cf88-5d43-b613-b0e7364d110e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449458} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4cb6286-7e1d-5d71-95bf-438c63766041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448838} {:answer/id #uuid "c7255ac2-2e51-5f09-8fcc-64d6f0d0e25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7255ac2-2e51-5f09-8fcc-64d6f0d0e25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448839} :answer-field/id #uuid "4da59e2a-2004-5754-a752-61aa8752b4c9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bef513f1-2d4e-54ff-9389-039c04a6d703" :question/math-academy-id "q-97051" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A+2I)=2$\n- **II.** $A+2I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+2I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+2I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A+2I$ is singular, then $(A+2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "436bf24b-bdf6-5bd1-bf14-e293249ddd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452431} {:answer/id #uuid "363b7103-4c5b-566a-8bb6-bb6db139809a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "363b7103-4c5b-566a-8bb6-bb6db139809a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452432} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed94cd18-02ea-5163-8871-843e8d20bacd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453063} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddb395a3-0026-5fe2-8672-0d8a776661b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452433} {:answer/id #uuid "ad49d154-32c7-51a1-9c40-23951e669167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddb395a3-0026-5fe2-8672-0d8a776661b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452433} :answer-field/id #uuid "85f07249-c02b-5278-9c34-c03c810c79e2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453064} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b316a2be-90ba-5f03-af54-19e6e2c49f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452435} {:answer/id #uuid "4beb83e8-81ac-5a00-996b-7891230847a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4beb83e8-81ac-5a00-996b-7891230847a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452436} :answer-field/id #uuid "75b5380c-d718-5a65-a9f5-9759ac621a6b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a9eef89-f664-5ecc-a524-f84c3e33cc1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97871" :question/problem "A matrix $B$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n- **III.** $B$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =1$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 1-2+1 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (2)^{4}-2(2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 16-8+1 & =0 \\\\ 9 & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $B$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b62692e4-70a9-5b64-b572-8e95101f299d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454262} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f96054-430f-5abb-9738-492912b4513f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2f96054-430f-5abb-9738-492912b4513f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454263} :answer-field/id #uuid "52fb6fd8-12c2-5a40-95db-e8d434005870" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454881} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43234e8f-f0ca-5794-8734-537c4540ced4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454264} {:answer/id #uuid "aea27afc-4d53-57d1-a1a6-faf52d441711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aea27afc-4d53-57d1-a1a6-faf52d441711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454265} :answer-field/id #uuid "f38bf0d7-cb37-587a-8201-a3fd186776a4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454882} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f198704-9c2b-52ba-ab29-315f0f4f7390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454266} {:answer/id #uuid "356b12b2-b30c-58ef-898f-2e04dbb2332f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "356b12b2-b30c-58ef-898f-2e04dbb2332f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454267} :answer-field/id #uuid "d51aab6a-c250-5230-a3e0-85d81f4fb26b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cf92ef9-ce4a-5cbb-9e9d-9e8a2989e53d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97944" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(B+2I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B+2I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B+2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=B+2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a8febcf-d8f8-5bb1-bd0f-5ea5157901db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454268} {:answer/id #uuid "0d0ab0d9-c27a-5f5c-af64-9667450caa58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a8febcf-d8f8-5bb1-bd0f-5ea5157901db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454268} :answer-field/id #uuid "8384c379-0806-5ddf-b3d0-e3b82670fa9b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26764f15-21bd-5865-b436-a36b9b696003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454270} {:answer/id #uuid "a796bded-1ab7-53dd-8b24-5a4f06d7d263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a796bded-1ab7-53dd-8b24-5a4f06d7d263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454271} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a6693f4-1188-5c06-b9f9-4c671b8a0599" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454885} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f7d45de-1453-5098-a4a6-edffb970d0d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454272} {:answer/id #uuid "89362b6b-2fd1-52b4-a6a5-5b6f4d773b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89362b6b-2fd1-52b4-a6a5-5b6f4d773b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454273} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ba61e62-3f3b-5078-93dc-7f400ec2f0c3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f636785f-61ec-504d-b842-394a5a79199b" :question/math-academy-id "q-97947" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(C-2I)x=0$ has only zero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-2I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beea7466-c41d-589d-865f-48504bd7909f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448852} {:answer/id #uuid "155e2c10-fe31-5b66-8f91-aef1c4a7653e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "beea7466-c41d-589d-865f-48504bd7909f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448852} :answer-field/id #uuid "14c928b0-156c-536e-bed2-6f5b5e4401fc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449466} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46813a85-d060-55df-bb6c-d675e5cbd606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448854} {:answer/id #uuid "cd88e49f-fa58-5c5b-9e14-f03a5d794525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46813a85-d060-55df-bb6c-d675e5cbd606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448854} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2f9766b-60ff-519d-951a-b93e45549f26" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449467} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92227e0d-7a0b-5d15-9e0d-3258bf7187f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448856} {:answer/id #uuid "069779b3-0176-5660-81d6-ff6ef90ecab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "069779b3-0176-5660-81d6-ff6ef90ecab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448857} :answer-field/id #uuid "46bf0043-aa6e-51a8-975c-6599d0da478c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b68a0678-9348-57e6-b4b3-d9e83bab09ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-97961" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A+I$ is a non-singular matrix\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A+I)={R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II is false. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+I)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "775c219f-a459-55cb-a155-865e3fee7ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452467} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffb7501-a137-566a-82b1-7897016fb94f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ffb7501-a137-566a-82b1-7897016fb94f" :answer/type {:db/i